msgid "A free Estonian lesson on a noun’s three main forms, põhivormid (kool, kooli, kooli; pood, poe, poodi): how a dictionary and Sõnaveeb list them, and how most words in -ne and -us, and one-syllable words ending in a long vowel, show the third. Every word is read aloud."
msgstr "A free Estonian lesson on a noun’s three main forms, põhivormid (kool, kooli, kooli; pood, poe, poodi): how a dictionary and Sõnaveeb list them, and how most words in -ne and -us, and one-syllable words ending in a long vowel, show the third. Every word is read aloud."

msgid "A free Estonian lesson on four case endings: -ga for with and by (sõbraga, bussiga), -ta for without (piimata), -ni for until and as far as (kella viieni, Tartuni) and -na for as (arstina), each on the genitive. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free Estonian lesson on four case endings: -ga for with and by (sõbraga, bussiga), -ta for without (piimata), -ni for until and as far as (kella viieni, Tartuni) and -na for as (arstina), each on the genitive. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free Estonian lesson on meeldima, “to like”: mulle meeldib and the other persons, meeldivad for more than one thing, meeldib with a verb, ei meeldi, questions with kas and kellele, and feelings such as mul on külm. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free Estonian lesson on meeldima, “to like”: mulle meeldib and the other persons, meeldivad for more than one thing, meeldib with a verb, ei meeldi, questions with kas and kellele, and feelings such as mul on külm. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free Estonian lesson on personal pronouns in their cases: minuga and sinuta on the genitive, the short forms mulle, mul and mult, the partitive mind, sind, teda, and telling minu, mul and mind apart. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free Estonian lesson on personal pronouns in their cases: minuga and sinuta on the genitive, the short forms mulle, mul and mult, the partitive mind, sind, teda, and telling minu, mul and mind apart. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free Estonian lesson on personal pronouns: mina, sina, tema and the rest in their long and short forms, and the verb olema, to be. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free Estonian lesson on personal pronouns: mina, sina, tema and the rest in their long and short forms, and the verb olema, to be. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free Estonian lesson on telling the time: full hours, pool, veerand and kolmveerand, which name the hour that’s coming, and minutes past and to. Every time is read aloud."
msgstr "A free Estonian lesson on telling the time: full hours, pool, veerand and kolmveerand, which name the hour that’s coming, and minutes past and to. Every time is read aloud."

msgid "A free Estonian pronunciation lesson: the nine vowels with õ, ä, ö and ü, stress on the first syllable, and how a double letter changes a word. Every word is read aloud."
msgstr "A free Estonian pronunciation lesson: the nine vowels with õ, ä, ö and ü, stress on the first syllable, and how a double letter changes a word. Every word is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on consonant gradation in Estonian: why jalg becomes jala and tuba becomes toa, which forms take the strong or the weak stem, the four ways a consonant weakens (kapp, kapi; pank, panga; pood, poe; lind, linnu), words that go the other way (mõte, mõtte), and the same change in verbs (lugema, loen). Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on consonant gradation in Estonian: why jalg becomes jala and tuba becomes toa, which forms take the strong or the weak stem, the four ways a consonant weakens (kapp, kapi; pank, panga; pood, poe; lind, linnu), words that go the other way (mõte, mõtte), and the same change in verbs (lugema, loen). Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on Estonian modal verbs: oskan for what you know how to do, saan for what nothing stops you doing, võin and tohin for what is allowed, pean, tuleb and mul on vaja for what you have to do, and how to say can’t, mustn’t and don’t have to. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on Estonian modal verbs: oskan for what you know how to do, saan for what nothing stops you doing, võin and tohin for what is allowed, pean, tuleb and mul on vaja for what you have to do, and how to say can’t, mustn’t and don’t have to. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on Estonian negation in the present tense: ei before the verb, the same for every person, ei ole and pole, and how to answer a question with no. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on Estonian negation in the present tense: ei before the verb, the same for every person, ei ole and pole, and how to answer a question with no. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on Estonian noun stems: why pood becomes poes and tuba becomes toas, words that add a vowel (koolis, raamatus), words that stay the same (kodus), and how a dictionary shows the stem. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on Estonian noun stems: why pood becomes poes and tuba becomes toas, words that add a vowel (koolis, raamatus), words that stay the same (kodus), and how a dictionary shows the stem. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on Estonian numbers: zero to ten, -teist and -kümmend, hundreds and thousands, and numbers heard in prices, ages and phone numbers. Every number is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on Estonian numbers: zero to ten, -teist and -kümmend, hundreds and thousands, and numbers heard in prices, ages and phone numbers. Every number is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on Estonian ordinal numbers and dates: esimene, teine, kolmas and how the rest are built from the genitive, the months, asking mis kuupäev täna on?, writing 21. november, and -l for on a date or a floor (viiendal mail, kolmandal korrusel). Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on Estonian ordinal numbers and dates: esimene, teine, kolmas and how the rest are built from the genitive, the months, asking mis kuupäev täna on?, writing 21. november, and -l for on a date or a floor (viiendal mail, kolmandal korrusel). Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on Estonian questions: kas for yes or no, answers with jah and ei, the question words kes, mis, kus, millal, miks, kuidas and kui palju, and three everyday questions to learn whole. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on Estonian questions: kas for yes or no, answers with jah and ei, the question words kes, mis, kus, millal, miks, kuidas and kui palju, and three everyday questions to learn whole. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on Estonian verbs in the present tense: the -ma infinitive, the stem, and the six endings -n, -d, -b, -me, -te and -vad on ten everyday verbs. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on Estonian verbs in the present tense: the -ma infinitive, the stem, and the six endings -n, -d, -b, -me, -te and -vad on ten everyday verbs. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on Estonian’s three “inside” cases, the inessive, illative and elative: kus, kuhu and kust, -s for in, -sse for into (Tartusse) and -st for out of (koolist), the short forms kooli, poodi and koju, and how to say where you’re from. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on Estonian’s three “inside” cases, the inessive, illative and elative: kus, kuhu and kust, -s for in, -sse for into (Tartusse) and -st for out of (koolist), the short forms kooli, poodi and koju, and how to say where you’re from. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on Estonian’s three “on” cases, the adessive, allative and ablative: kus, kuhu and kust, -l for on and at (laual, tööl), -le for onto and to (lauale, tööle) and -lt for off and from (laualt, töölt), which places take -le and which -sse, and -le and -lt on a person (emale, emalt). Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on Estonian’s three “on” cases, the adessive, allative and ablative: kus, kuhu and kust, -l for on and at (laual, tööl), -le for onto and to (lauale, tööle) and -lt for off and from (laualt, töölt), which places take -le and which -sse, and -le and -lt on a person (emale, emalt). Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on saying “I have” in Estonian: mul, sul, tal, meil, teil and neil on, names with -l such as Maril on, the question kellel on?, mul on vaja for I need, and how to answer with jah, on or ei ole. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on saying “I have” in Estonian: mul, sul, tal, meil, teil and neil on, names with -l such as Maril on, the question kellel on?, mul on vaja for I need, and how to answer with jah, on or ei ole. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on saying where you are in Estonian: -s for in (Tallinnas, koolis), -l for on and at (laual, tööl), which places take which, and how to answer kus, “where”. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on saying where you are in Estonian: -s for in (Tallinnas, koolis), -l for on and at (laual, tööl), which places take which, and how to answer kus, “where”. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on suggesting and allowing in Estonian: let’s with the present’s me form without me (teeme, vaatame; minema gives lähme), lähme with the -ma infinitive (lähme ujuma), and las with the present (las ta tuleb, las ma aitan). Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on suggesting and allowing in Estonian: let’s with the present’s me form without me (teeme, vaatame; minema gives lähme), lähme with the -ma infinitive (lähme ujuma), and las with the present (las ta tuleb, las ma aitan). Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian -ma, -mas and -mast forms: lähen ujuma (going swimming), olen ujumas (out swimming) and tulen ujumast (back from swimming). They answer kuhu?, kus? and kust? as places do. Also when to say lähen, käin or tulen. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian -ma, -mas and -mast forms: lähen ujuma (going swimming), olen ujumas (out swimming) and tulen ujumast (back from swimming). They answer kuhu?, kus? and kust? as places do. Also when to say lähen, käin or tulen. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian conditional, the would form: -ks with the person endings (ma läheksin, sa läheksid, ta läheks), ei läheks for every person, polite requests (ma tahaksin kohvi, kas sa saaksid aidata?) and if clauses (kui mul oleks aega, läheksin kinno). Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian conditional, the would form: -ks with the person endings (ma läheksin, sa läheksid, ta läheks), ei läheks for every person, polite requests (ma tahaksin kohvi, kas sa saaksid aidata?) and if clauses (kui mul oleks aega, läheksin kinno). Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian days of the week: the seven names, the four with numbers in them, the one-letter days on opening hours, and täna, homme and eile. Every word is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian days of the week: the seven names, the four with numbers in them, the one-letter days on opening hours, and täna, homme and eile. Every word is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian genitive case: minu, sinu, tema, meie, teie and nende for my, your, his or her, our and their, the owner before the noun (Mari raamat), kelle for whose, and the genitive as the form that takes -s and -l (kooli, koolis). Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian genitive case: minu, sinu, tema, meie, teie and nende for my, your, his or her, our and their, the owner before the noun (Mari raamat), kelle for whose, and the genitive as the form that takes -s and -l (kooli, koolis). Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian imperative: the verb’s stem as a command (loe!, istu!, tule siia!), ära for don’t, the -ge and -ke forms for more than one person or polite you, everyday commands such as räägi aeglasemalt, palun, and a short conversation at a café. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian imperative: the verb’s stem as a command (loe!, istu!, tule siia!), ära for don’t, the -ge and -ke forms for more than one person or polite you, everyday commands such as räägi aeglasemalt, palun, and a short conversation at a café. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian impersonal, for what people do with no person named: building it from the -tud form (räägitud, räägitakse, räägiti), the verbs to learn by heart (tehakse, süüakse, minnakse), saying no (ei räägita, ei räägitud) and the case of the object. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian impersonal, for what people do with no person named: building it from the -tud form (räägitud, räägitakse, räägiti), the verbs to learn by heart (tehakse, süüakse, minnakse), saying no (ei räägita, ei räägitud) and the case of the object. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian object: the partitive for part of something, an action still going on, after ei and after verbs such as ootama and otsima; the genitive for the whole thing done (Ma lugesin raamatu läbi); and the nominative in commands (Osta pilet!). Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian object: the partitive for part of something, an action still going on, after ei and after verbs such as ootama and otsima; the genitive for the whole thing done (Ma lugesin raamatu läbi); and the nominative in commands (Osta pilet!). Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian partitive case, a noun’s third form: after numbers from two up (kaks kassi, viis eurot), after mitu and amounts (tass kohvi), after pole (mul pole kassi) and with food and drink (ma joon kohvi). Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian partitive case, a noun’s third form: after numbers from two up (kaks kassi, viis eurot), after mitu and amounts (tass kohvi), after pole (mul pole kassi) and with food and drink (ma joon kohvi). Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian past tense (lihtminevik): -si- on most verbs (elasin, lugesin, rääkisin), -i- on a few everyday ones (olin, läksin, sõin), the endings that say who did it, and eile and eelmisel nädalal. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian past tense (lihtminevik): -si- on most verbs (elasin, lugesin, rääkisin), -i- on a few everyday ones (olin, läksin, sõin), the endings that say who did it, and eile and eelmisel nädalal. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian perfect and pluperfect: the -nud form (lugenud, käinud, läinud), olen with it for what has happened (ma olen Tartus käinud), olin for what had happened before (oli juba läinud), and when to use the simple past instead (eile käisin Tartus). Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian perfect and pluperfect: the -nud form (lugenud, käinud, läinud), olen with it for what has happened (ma olen Tartus käinud), olin for what had happened before (oli juba läinud), and when to use the simple past instead (eile käisin Tartus). Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian plural cases: build the plural genitive, usually from the partitive (majade, raamatute, inimeste), then add the endings you know: majades, inimestele, sõpradega. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian plural cases: build the plural genitive, usually from the partitive (majade, raamatute, inimeste), then add the endings you know: majades, inimestele, sõpradega. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian plural partitive, the partitive for more than one: palju raamatuid, poes on õunu, mul ei ole lapsi; its endings -id, -sid and a changed last vowel (raamatuid, autosid, maju, koole); and how it differs from kaks raamatut and the plural raamatud. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian plural partitive, the partitive for more than one: palju raamatuid, poes on õunu, mul ei ole lapsi; its endings -id, -sid and a changed last vowel (raamatuid, autosid, maju, koole); and how it differs from kaks raamatut and the plural raamatud. Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the Estonian plural: the second form plus -d (kassid, raamatud), the stem change it keeps (poed, toad, mehed), the verb with a plural noun (lapsed loevad), and adjectives in the plural (suured majad). Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the Estonian plural: the second form plus -d (kassid, raamatud), the stem change it keeps (poed, toad, mehed), the verb with a plural noun (lapsed loevad), and adjectives in the plural (suured majad). Every sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on the three forms of an Estonian verb, lugema, lugeda and loen: how the -da infinitive is made with -da, -ta, -la, -na or -a, including teha, näha, süüa and juua, and the -ge and -ke commands built on it. Every word and sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on the three forms of an Estonian verb, lugema, lugeda and loen: how the -da infinitive is made with -da, -ta, -la, -na or -a, including teha, näha, süüa and juua, and the -ge and -ke commands built on it. Every word and sentence is read aloud."

msgid "A free lesson on which Estonian verbs take the -da infinitive after them and which take the -ma infinitive: tahan lugeda but pean lugema, with võin, saan, oskan, tohin, proovin, armastan and mulle meeldib on one side and hakkan, lähen, tule and jään on the other. Every sentence is read aloud."
msgstr "A free lesson on which Estonian verbs take the -da infinitive after them and which take the -ma infinitive: tahan lugeda but pean lugema, with võin, saan, oskan, tohin, proovin, armastan and mulle meeldib on one side and hakkan, lähen, tule and jään on the other. Every sentence is read aloud."

